    "q-163572": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163572",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $2\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $1\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest.",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14218054/assets/a8c9778580276589ff7a047ca3c7b620de842efd7b3799a70b6e4484bc5fa0e6.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{7})^{2} & =-2(1)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =14+2 \\\\ C & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=16-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =16-2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =16-2{x}^{3} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
